ananicy-rs for Debian
====================

This package builds the ananicy-rs daemon with cargo, its default feature set,
and installs the binary, the daemon's own systemd unit, shell completions and
the project documentation. The packaging itself lives in `contrib/debian/` in
the upstream repository; `debian/README.md` there is the maintainer's guide to
building it. This file is the end user's and the reviewer's.

Building
--------

This package needs rustc 1.88 or newer. That is higher than edition 2024
requires: the daemon uses let chains, which stabilised in 1.88, and an older
compiler will fail with `error[E0658]: let expressions in this position are
unstable` rather than telling you the version is too old. So this package cannot
be built against Debian stable, whose rustc is 1.85.1; it targets
trixie-backports or sid.

The source package is the git tree of the release tag plus a vendored copy of
every crate in `Cargo.lock`, in `debian/vendor.tar.xz`. That is what makes the
build reproducible: nothing is fetched from crates.io while the `.deb` is built.
Regenerate the archive with `debian/make-vendor.sh` whenever `Cargo.lock`
changes.

The build needs no network and no root. `Rules-Requires-Root: no` in
`debian/control` is not aspirational: `cargo build` and `cargo test` are the only
steps that run code, and neither needs privileges for this package's default
feature set. The tests in `ananicy-platform` that do need root, or a writable cgroup
hierarchy, detect that they are unprivileged and skip themselves with a message
rather than failing.

What is not built
-----------------

The `bpf` feature is off. It is not upstream's default, it would add `clang`,
`libbpf` and `rustfmt` to Build-Depends, and it needs `CAP_BPF` and `CAP_PERFMON`
at run time, which the shipped unit does not grant. The netlink process-event
source that this package does build is the one the unit's
`RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_NETLINK` and `CapabilityBoundingSet` are
written for.

Rules
-----

`ananicy-rs` ships no rules. It reads `*.rules`, `*.types` and `*.cgroups` from
`/etc/ananicy.d`, recursively and sorted by path, and creates that directory and
a default `ananicy.conf` on its first start if they are missing.

Neither this package nor `debian/control` declares a dependency on a rule set,
because none is packaged in Debian or its derivatives yet. Install one by hand
from <https://gitlab.com/ananicy-cpp/ananicy-cpp> or
<https://github.com/Nefelim4ag/Ananicy> before enabling the service:

    systemctl enable --now ananicy-rs.service

Configuration and upgrades
--------------------------

Nothing under `/etc` is a conffile, and nothing is added on upgrade. The
daemon owns the contents of `/etc/ananicy.d` and writes `ananicy.conf` itself;
packaging that file would both shadow the daemon's own default and make dpkg
reconcile a file the administrator is meant to own. `/etc/ananicy.d` itself is
packaged empty so the path exists before the first start.

systemd
-------

The unit is `/usr/lib/systemd/system/ananicy-rs.service`, generated from the
project's own `data/ananicy-rs.service.in`. Its hardening, `Nice=-5`,
`OOMScoreAdjust=-999`, `Delegate=yes` and `CapabilityBoundingSet` are upstream's
and are unchanged by the packaging. See
`/usr/share/doc/ananicy-rs/docs/SYSTEMD.md` for what each directive does and why
the daemon needs it.

Installing this package registers the unit; it does not enable or start it
(`dh_installsystemd --no-enable --no-start`), which matches the Fedora recipe
under `contrib/fedora/`. Enabling it is a deliberate act:

    systemctl enable --now ananicy-rs.service

An upgrade stops and restarts the service if it was running, so a running daemon
picks up the new executable.

No man page
-----------

The project has no man page, so this package has none, and `docs/CLI.md` plus
`docs/CONFIGURATION.md` are installed under `/usr/share/doc/ananicy-rs/docs/`
instead. `lintian` will report `no-manual-page-for-binary` for
`/usr/bin/ananicy-rs`; that tag is expected here. Writing a man page inside the
packaging would create a second copy of the command-line reference to keep in
step with the binary, and it belongs upstream, next to the code it documents.

Before any upload
-----------------

`debian/control` and `debian/changelog` carry a placeholder
`ananicy-rs-packaging@example.invalid` address. Replace it with a real
maintainer address and set `Standards-Version` to the current one.
